{"name":"Rustling Leaves","hex":"#ad6961","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rustling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of rustle"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The rounding up and theft of animals, cattle in particular."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rustling"},{"term":"leaves","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of leaf","from leave:\nplural of leave \nplural of leave"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of leave"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaves"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cattle raiding","blocks":[{"text":"Cattle raiding is the act of stealing live cattle, often several or many at once. In Australia, such stealing is often referred to as duffing and the perpetrator as a duffer. In parts of the country, however, especially in Queensland, the practice is known as poddy-dodging and the perpetrator as a poddy-dodger. In North America, especially in the Western cowboy culture, cattle theft is dubbed rustling and one who engages in it is a rustler."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Schw%C3%A4bischer_Bund_Luzerner_Schilling.jpg/330px-Schw%C3%A4bischer_Bund_Luzerner_Schilling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cattle_raiding"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_Image_of_Irelande_-_plate02.jpg/500px-The_Image_of_Irelande_-_plate02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_Image_of_Irelande_-_plate02.jpg/500px-The_Image_of_Irelande_-_plate02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of cattle raid in Ireland c. 1580 in The Image of Irelande by John Derricke.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Image_of_Irelande_-_plate02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/The_Great_Cattle_Raid_at_Harrison%27s_Landing.jpg/500px-The_Great_Cattle_Raid_at_Harrison%27s_Landing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/The_Great_Cattle_Raid_at_Harrison%27s_Landing.jpg/1280px-The_Great_Cattle_Raid_at_Harrison%27s_Landing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/The_Great_Cattle_Raid_at_Harrison%27s_Landing.jpg/500px-The_Gre